\begin{defn}
Phylogenetic trees, $T_1=(V_1, E_1, \varphi_1)$ and  $T_2=(V_2, E_2, \varphi_2)$ on a taxon set $X$, are \emph{isomorphic} if there exists a bijective map $f : V_1 \rightarrow V_2$, called an \emph{isomorphism}, such that if $\{u,v\} \in E_1$ then $\{f (u), f (v)\} \in E_2$ and  for every  $x \in X$ we have $\varphi_2 (x)=f (\varphi_1 (x))$ . 
\end{defn}

