\begin{proof}
Let $Q$ be a  linked system of quartet trees on a tree $T$ on a taxon set $X$. Linked systems are of size $n-3$ and each quartet tree distinguishes a unique edge in $T$. Let $T$ be a binary phylogenetic tree on a taxon set $X$ and let $e_1$ be an interior edge adjacent to a cherry. The tree is connected, which implies we can label the remaining interior edges $\{e_2, \cdots, e_{n-3} \}$ such that $e_j$ is adjacent to some $e_i$ with $i <j$. Moreover, because the support of each pair of quartet trees $\{q_i,q_j\}$ that distinguishes adjacent edges $\{e_i,e_j\}$ is five, each pair of quartet trees shares three taxa and each additional quartet tree in $Q$ adds one new taxon to the support of $Q$. Thus, linked systems meet the criteria in Proposition \ref{bocker2} and are definitive.
\end{proof}

